course: dictionary
lesson: 1457
title: A rare word with two meanings: casque
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A casque is a rare word, and it can mean two different things. Look at the words around it to find out which meaning is right. «The southern cassowary is a huge flightless bird with a casque on its head.» The words bird and on its head are your clues. Something on a bird's head is not a helmet, so here casque means a hard lump.
explain: In a history story, casque usually means a helmet. Knights and soldiers long ago wore a metal casque to protect their heads in battle. So if a story talks about a knight and a battle, the clues point to the helmet meaning.
explain: Always ask: what would fit in this sentence? Then swap your meaning in and check. «The cassowary has a hard lump on its head» still makes sense, so the lump meaning is right.
fact: Casque is a noun. A casque is a thing, and casques is the plural.
fact: The first meaning is an old word for a metal helmet worn by knights and soldiers.
fact: The second meaning is a hard, bony or horny lump on the head or beak of some birds.
fact: Birds with a casque include the cassowary and the hornbill.
fact: «The cassowary can be dangerous to people who get close.» Read the whole sentence to find the clues you need.
exercise: «The cassowary has a casque on its head.» What is a casque here? | *A hard lump on the head of a bird | A pair of wings | A kind of sandwich | A long, sharp tail
exercise: «The knight put on his casque before the battle.» What is a casque here? | A sword belt | A horse's saddle | *A metal helmet | A soft cushion
exercise: Find and copy the word that tells you where the casque is: «The southern cassowary is a huge flightless bird with a casque on its head.» | *head | huge | bird | flightless
test: «The cassowary's casque is hard and bony.» Which meaning fits best? | A knight's sword | *A hard lump on the head of a bird | A soft pillow | A kind of cake
test: Why would a nature book use the word casque instead of helmet? | Because casque means a feather | Because helmet is always wrong in every sentence | Because casque means a big bird | *Because casque is the correct word for the hard lump on a bird's head or beak
exam: «A knight's casque clanged as he rode into battle.» What does casque mean here? | A type of horse | *A metal helmet | A castle wall | A bird's beak
